+# Decision: How committed board changes reach browsers
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard shows one board per market event, used by up to 800 staff browsers at once. Editors submit changes over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed change one authoritative event revision; browsers display the board as of some revision. One committed revision must reach every connected browser, whichever route it travels.
+
+The project must choose that route, and the choice is not neutral for the rest of the system. The route determines what the change store must keep, what the delivery layer owns, and what the browser must be able to do — so it is settled before those three nodes are designed.
+
+Three routes are credible at the pilot's scale and against the pilot's goals:
+
+- **Five-second HTTPS polling.** The browser asks for the board every five seconds and replaces what it has with the answer.
+- **Self-hosted WebSocket connections.** The server holds an open WebSocket connection to each browser and pushes each committed revision down it.
+- **A hosted realtime service.** A managed pub/sub provider carries the push path between the project and the browsers.
+
+Two boundaries apply to every option. The freshness goal is that a connected browser shows a committed change within 15 seconds; subsecond updates would be useful but are not required, so no option is eliminated by latency alone. And the board carries stall identifiers and operational statuses — closures, late arrivals, power faults — not customer records or payment details, so no option may put customer data on a third party's path.
+
+Nothing about the choice follows from the fact that the pilot runs on infrastructure the team already operates. That is a preference to be weighed against the alternatives, not a constraint: a team can run a WebSocket tier, and can equally buy one from a provider.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+The route is judged against the following elements, listed with their importance. Each element is something the route can be strong or weak at, so each one can move the selection.
+
+- **Update latency headroom** (moderate) — how much faster than the 15-second requirement the route can deliver, judged by whether a committed revision reaches a connected browser in roughly a second rather than in seconds. Latency beyond the requirement is worth something, but the requirement is met by all three routes, so this element cannot carry the decision alone.
+- **Vendor dependency and portability** (major) — how much of the delivery path the project does not own, and what a later move to a different route would cost. This is the element the project's tenet **Own over rent, within reason** governs: where a capability can be self-hosted at an acceptable operational cost, the project leans toward operating it itself.
+- **Operational burden carried by the team** (major) — the ongoing work the project takes on: connection capacity, session lifecycle, keep-alives, reconnection, catch-up, and the failure modes of each.
+- **Recurring cost at pilot scale** (moderate) — what the route costs per event, per connected browser, and per market season, counting both vendor fees and the labour to keep the route running.
+- **Fan-out cost under opening-time bursts** (moderate) — how the route behaves when 20 edits per second must reach 800 browsers, and whether that cost is paid per browser, per message, or not at all.
+- **Recovery after a browser is disconnected** (moderate) — how a browser that has been away for up to 20 minutes is brought back to the current board, and how much machinery that takes.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+The browser asks the server for the board every five seconds over ordinary HTTPS. The server answers with the board as of a revision, and the browser replaces what it holds with the answer. Nothing is pushed, no connection is held open between requests, and a failed poll is simply a poll to be made again. Committed changes become visible at the next poll.
+
+- Update latency headroom: **weak** fit — every committed change lags by up to five seconds by construction.
+- Vendor dependency and portability: **strong** fit — no third party sits on the path, and the browser talks only to the project's own server.
+- Operational burden: **strong** fit — there is nothing to keep alive between requests; connections are ordinary request lifetimes the platform already handles.
+- Recurring cost: **partial** fit — no per-connection or per-message fees, but 800 browsers polling every five seconds is roughly 160 requests a second, each of which re-reads or re-serializes board state.
+- Fan-out under bursts: **partial** fit — a burst of edits creates no per-browser spike, but the read floor does not shrink when the market is quiet, so the cost is paid continuously rather than in proportion to activity.
+- Recovery after disconnect: **strong** fit — a returning browser asks once more and is current within one poll interval.
+
+*Summary:* the simplest and cheapest route, at the price of a five-second lag on every change and constant read traffic while the board is idle.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSocket connections
+
+The server opens a WebSocket connection to each connected browser and pushes each committed event revision down it as the revision is committed. The browser holds the connection open, applies revisions as they arrive, and remembers the last revision it applied. When a connection drops, the browser reconnects and asks for everything committed since that revision, and the server replays those revisions from the stored log.
+
+- Update latency headroom: **strong** fit — revisions reach the browser as they are committed, so the lag is network time rather than a poll interval.
+- Vendor dependency and portability: **strong** fit — the project owns the socket tier and the protocol, and nothing sits between the browser and the project.
+- Operational burden: **weak** fit — the project owns connection capacity for 800 browsers, session lifecycle, keep-alives, and the reconnect and catch-up behaviour of every browser. Each of those failure modes is now the project's to detect and repair.
+- Recurring cost: **partial** fit — infrastructure and bandwidth costs scale with connected browsers, with no provider's per-connection or per-message fee on top.
+- Fan-out under bursts: **partial** fit — a committed revision is fanned out to every connected browser by the project's own tier, so 20 edits a second across 800 browsers is on the order of 16,000 sends a second for the project to size and plan for.
+- Recovery after disconnect: **partial** fit — a returning browser needs a catch-up path, the server needs replay from the revision log, and the browser needs to track the last revision it applied.
+
+*Summary:* fast updates with no vendor on the path, at the price of owning connection capacity, reconnection, and catch-up.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A managed realtime provider carries the push path. The project's server publishes committed revisions to a provider channel, and browsers subscribe to that channel through the provider's client library and receive revisions as they are published. The provider holds the connections and absorbs the fan-out.
+
+- Update latency headroom: **strong** fit — published revisions reach browsers through the provider's network in roughly a second.
+- Vendor dependency and portability: **weak** fit — a provider's client library, channel model, and publish semantics sit in the middle of the delivery path, and delivery becomes a wrapper around a provider API rather than a service the project designs and owns.
+- Operational burden: **strong** fit — the provider owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect handling; the project publishes revisions and consumes them.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** fit — the provider bills per connection and per message, and both figures scale with the number of connected browsers and the number of revisions committed.
+- Fan-out under bursts: **strong** fit — the fan-out is the provider's to absorb, on infrastructure the project does not size.
+- Recovery after disconnect: **partial** fit — reconnect and re-subscribe are handled by the provider's client library, but the browser must still be told what it missed, which the project supplies as a catch-up read.
+
+*Summary:* the least work and the least risk in the connection layer, at the price of recurring fees, provider coupling, and a delivery path the project does not control.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSocket connections** are selected.
+
+The choice turns on **vendor dependency and portability**, which is the element the tenet **Own over rent, within reason** governs. With the operational cost judged acceptable at the pilot's scale, the project prefers to operate the delivery path rather than buy it, and on that element polling and self-hosted sockets both fit strongly while the hosted service fits weakly.
+
+The choice is taken **against** the element **operational burden carried by the team**: polling is the lightest option and the hosted service is nearly as light, while self-hosted sockets ask the project to own connection capacity, session lifecycle, reconnection, and catch-up, and the project accepts that work. It is likewise taken against **recurring cost at pilot scale** and **fan-out cost under opening-time bursts**, where the hosted service's per-connection and per-message billing and its offloaded fan-out are genuine advantages the project gives up. It is not taken against **update latency headroom**, where polling fits weakly and the selected route fits strongly.
+
+What the choice forecloses: the project does not get a provider's connection infrastructure, its operational dashboard, or its protection against misbehaving clients, and a reconnect storm during opening time is the project's to absorb. The route also cannot be swapped for a hosted service later without reworking the store's publication path and the browser's subscription model, so the choice is worth revisiting only if the operational cost of the connection tier proves larger than acceptable.
+
+What the choice obliges downstream:
+
+- **Change store** — keep a replayable log of event revisions, and publish each committed revision reliably to delivery, so that a failure between storing a revision and announcing it cannot lose that revision.
+- **Delivery** — own the socket session lifecycle: accepting connections, keeping them alive, expiring them, and serving catch-up replays to a reconnecting browser.
+- **Browser sync** — track the last revision applied, reconnect after a dropped connection, and replay what was missed, so that a browser which was away converges on the same board as one that never disconnected.
