 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSocket delivery.** Committed changes are pushed to browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections. Five-second HTTPS polling would have kept delivery stateless and request/response, at the cost of repeated reads and a few seconds of lag on every change; the store would have needed cursor queries and browsers a polling loop. A hosted realtime service would have given the quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection operations to run, at the cost of recurring fees and provider coupling; the store would have needed publication reconciliation and delivery a provider wrapper. Self-hosted sockets place connection capacity, replay, and reconnection work on this project, and that operational cost is accepted in exchange for fast updates with no realtime vendor dependency. The full weighing of the three routes against the decision's elements is recorded in `docs/decisions/delivery-route.md`.
